**************************************************
* Introduction                                   *
**************************************************
This notebook is a fork of KACTL. kactl.pdf is built from the LaTeX in
content/ plus the source files themselves: every chapter is a directory with a
chapter.tex that \kactlimports its sources, and a Python preprocessor turns
each source file into the LaTeX fragment that gets typeset (name line,
Description/Usage/Time block, hash + line count, code listing).

Worth knowing where things are:
	content/kactl.tex		cover data, margins, list of chapters
	content/tex/kactlpkg.sty	every layout macro
	content/tex/preprocessor.py	source file -> LaTeX, and the page headers
	content/contest/hash.sh		the hash printed with each template
	doc/scripts/			test/CI scripts (see doc/scripts/README)
	stress-tests/			the tests that 'make test' runs
	doc/audit-notes.md		how the pipeline works and what bites

doc/example.h shows how a source file is annotated.


**************************************************
* System requirements                            *
**************************************************
	pdflatex, run with -shell-escape (the Makefile does). Beyond
	texlive-latex-base and texlive-latex-recommended it needs
		texlive-latex-extra	tocloft, enumitem, pmboxdraw
		texlive-fonts-extra	DejaVuSansMono, the code font
		cm-super		the Type1 T1-encoded Computer Modern the
					prose is set in; without it pdflatex
					falls back to bitmap fonts
	python3			runs the preprocessor via \write18
	gcc			hash.sh pipes through gcc's 'cpp'
	coreutils, bash		rm, tr, md5sum, cut; hash.sh needs bash
	GNU make

Built here with pdfTeX 3.141592653-2.6-1.40.25 (TeX Live 2023), Python 3.12,
GCC 13. Everything goes through \write18 and Unix tools, so on Windows build
inside WSL.


**************************************************
* Building                                       *
**************************************************
Run make from the repo root: TEXINPUTS, the preprocessor and hash.sh all use
relative paths, and the page-header state file header.tmp is written to the
current directory.

	make fast	one pdflatex pass, ~7 s. The table of contents and the
			page numbers come from the previous run's
			build/kactl.toc and build/kactl.aux, so they can lag
			one run behind.
	make kactl	two passes, ~14 s, plus test-session.pdf. Use this for
			anything you print or commit.

Both leave the result in build/ and copy it to kactl.pdf. 'make help' lists
the other targets (test, test-compiles, showexcluded, widelines, clean,
veryclean).


**************************************************
* When the build fails                           *
**************************************************
pdflatex runs with -halt-on-error, so it stops at the first error instead of
asking questions; the message is the line starting with '!', in the terminal
or in build/kactl.log.

Anything the preprocessor rejects comes back as \kactlerror{...}, which fails
the build with the file name in the message. To see what one file turns into,
run the preprocessor by hand from the repo root:

	python3 content/tex/preprocessor.py -i content/graph/Dinic.h
	python3 content/tex/preprocessor.py -i content/contest/template.cpp -l rawcpp

The fragment goes to stdout (-o writes a file). The language comes from the
extension unless -l overrides it, exactly like the [-l ...] option of
\kactlimport in a chapter.tex. Running it by hand also appends the template
name to ./header.tmp; the next build deletes that file at \begin{document}.

If a run dies with '! Undefined control sequence' while reading
(build/kactl.aux), the stale .aux is calling something a package you removed
used to define:

	(build//kactl.aux
	! Undefined control sequence.
	l.8 \ttl@writefile
	                  {ptc}{\ttl@starttoc{chapters@1}}

'make clean' and rebuild.


**************************************************
* Adding new chapters                            *
**************************************************
Create a folder for the chapter inside the 'content' folder. Then create (or
copy) a chapter.tex file inside this new folder, which will be the main file
for the chapter. You can then place source files in this folder and include
them by modifying the chapter.tex file.
Finally, add the chapter to content/kactl.tex with the 'kactlchapter' command.
Ex. \kactlchapter{geometry} will include the file content/geometry/chapter.tex


**************************************************
* Adding new source files                        *
**************************************************
First, locate the folder for the chapter where you want to add the new source
file, or follow the instructions for creating a new chapter above. The source
files go inside this chapter folder, and have to be included by the
chapter.tex in the same folder.
Ex. \kactlimport{example.h} will include the file example.h from the same
folder; \kactlimport[-l raw]{notes.txt} passes extra options to the
preprocessor.

Remember to annotate the file correctly before including it (doc/example.h),
and don't forget to write a stress test for the code!
